{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-148287",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To determine how much Peter should pay for $3$ pounds of seeds, we first find the unit rate of dollars per pound and then multiply it by $3$ pounds.\n\nTo find the unit rate of dollars per pound, we divide the number of dollars, $\\frac{3}{5},$ by the number of pounds, $\\frac{1}{15},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{1}{15}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{1}{15} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{15}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 15}{5\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{5} \\\\  & =45÷5 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $9$ dollars per pound. To determine how much Peter should pay for $3$ pounds, we multiply the unit rate by $3$ pounds and get\n\n$$\n9\\times 3=27.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Peter must pay $27$ dollars for $3$ pounds of pumpkin seeds.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
